WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.720
These days, everyone's a critic.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.210
So if that's the case, how awful must a movie
be to actually get a zero percent rating on

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.580
Rotten Tomatoes?

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.580
There are bad movies, and then there are movies
so terrible that literally no one single reviewer

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.530
in the world liked them.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.850
Here's a look at some of the truly horrible
movies that earned a zero percent rating on

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.050
Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.430
A Thousand Words

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:26.939
Starring Eddie Murphy, the comedy A Thousand
Words was filmed back in 2008.

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:31.050
But it was so bad Paramount waited four entire
years to release it.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.130
When your own studio doesn't want anyone to
see the film they paid to make, you know you

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.620
have problems!

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.219
And critics agreed that it was awful, lambasting
the lame story about about a guy who's cursed

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.510
by a magic tree and will die if he talks too
much.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:49.850
One critic called the flop "a poorly conceived
and startlingly miscast comedy that works

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.670
to every one of its stars' weaknesses."

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.629
So… not good.

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:58.600
[Laughter, unintelligible words]

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.660
Bucky Larson: Born To Be A Star

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.770
Every so often, a movie comes along that's
so bad, so random, it's hard to imagine how

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.930
it was ever made in the first place.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.229
Bucky Larson: Born to Be a Star is one of
those movies.

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:17.740
The film, which starred Nick Swardson as a
dopey small-town grocery bagger who moves

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:23.259
to L.A. to follow his parents' pr0n-star footsteps,
opened in 2011 to some of the worst reviews

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:25.780
Hollywood had seen in decades.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.170
Many critics trashed the film for its unfunny,
childish jokes.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:34.009
In fact, reviews were so harsh, by the time
the movie opened, Swardson's leading-man career

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:35.360
was already over.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.609
A star was not born.

00:01:37.609 --> 00:01:39.429
“What the f--- just happened?”

00:01:39.429 --> 00:01:42.740
“My brain just sparkled!”

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.930
The Nutcracker In 3D

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:50.789
One of the most egregious offenders of the
post-Avatar 3D craze was 2010's notoriously

00:01:50.789 --> 00:01:56.859
awful The Nutcracker in 3D, which took Tchaikovsky's
ballet and turned it into a bombastic musical

00:01:56.859 --> 00:02:02.200
featuring Elle Fanning, Nathan Lane, and not
exactly subtle references to — wait for

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.329
it — the Holocaust.

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:09.030
Worst of all, the movie's over-the-top sets
and CGI were literal eyesores, looking less

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.629
like a ballet and more like Michael Bay.

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:15.750
Ultimately, Lou Lumenick of the New York Post
summed it up best when he called The Nutcracker

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:21.250
in 3D "spectacularly misconceived, bloated,
and incredibly ugly."

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:23.629
But tell us how you really feel, Lou.

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:26.069
Superbabies: Baby Geniuses 2

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:30.900
The original Baby Geniuses was just one positive
review away from making this list.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.580
That in itself is enough reason to wonder
how anyone on Earth allowed a sequel to happen.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:43.689
And yet, somehow, some way, Superbabies: Baby
Geniuses 2 rolled into theaters in 2004.

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:44.720
“No!

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:48.880
Look at that!

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.900
I warned you!”

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.959
“It’s playtime!”

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:57.800
Unsurprisingly, critics were quick to call
it out for being what it was: a dumb idea.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:03.290
Joanne Kaufman of the Wall Street Journal
called it "unspeakably ghastly" and J.R. Jones

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:08.269
of the Chicago Reader said it was "excruciating,"
while Christopher Borrelli of the Toledo Blade

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:13.500
pretty much summed up everyone's feeling when
he wrote, "I found this all profoundly disturbing."

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:15.600
“What?”

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:16.180
“The?”

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:16.860
“Heck?”

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:28.500
Undeterred, producers turned this series into
a full-fledged franchise, releasing three

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.840
more installments, but the critics just couldn't
take any more: none of the subsequent Baby

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:37.569
Geniuses even have enough reviews to earn
a spot on the Tomatometer.

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:39.000
One Missed Call

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.970
The modern revival of the horror movie genre
has led to a number of bad decisions in Hollywood,

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.629
like that time Paris Hilton co-starred in
a remake of House of Wax.

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:52.849
But the worst of the bunch was the ridiculous
One Missed Call, a remake of a 2003 Japanese

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:56.909
horror movie that opened to truly terrifying
reviews in 2008.

00:03:56.909 --> 00:04:01.520
A blatant rip off of the far superior Ring
films, critics were quick to roll their eyes

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.090
at the film's premise, which essentially revolved
around a cell phone that killed people, calling

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.970
it contrived, silly, and a complete waste
of time.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.349
And those were the nicer reviews.

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:13.810
This is one call you should be glad you missed.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:14.810
Thanks for watching!

